**Across all four reviewers—ZLCd, yYLm, KQLK, and 7Ejc—the primary concerns focus on:**

1) Computational efficiency and complexity of the proposed MPFT framework;

2) Theoretical foundations and guarantees of the Pareto-tracking mechanism;

3) Methodological clarity, especially regarding sparse-region definition, weight-adjustment, and “population-free” claims;

4) Comparisons with related paradigms (evolutionary MP-MORL, GPI-based, decomposition-based, and high-dimensional methods such as C-MORL and GPI-LS);

5) Scalability to high-dimensional objectives and discrete environments.

**We have fully addressed all concerns in the revised manuscript:**

**1. Efficient Computation \& Time Complexity.**
Reviewers questioned whether approximating Pareto-vertex policies, alternating ascent–reverse updates, or multi-stage design could be computationally expensive. We provided:

(1) A rigorous per-stage complexity analysis (Appendix D.4, Please refer to page 25), proving linear complexity in objective number;

(2) Empirical results showing 77.72\% fewer env steps vs. evolutionary baselines and significantly lower per-update cost;

(3) Additional experiments (Table 3, please refer to page 10; Tables 11-15, please refer to pages 35-37) demonstrating robustness under significantly reduced training budgets and single-policy tracking (SPFT) effectiveness.

**2. Theoretical Foundations \& Exponential Convergence.** Concerns on the scope of our theoretical guarantee and coverage of the Pareto front were addressed by:

(1) Clarifying that exponential convergence applies to local stability toward the Pareto-stationary set;

(2) Explaining how multi-track design (Stage 2 \& 3) ensures empirical full-front exploration, even under non-connected or discrete fronts;

(3) Providing precise definitions (e.g., continuity, sparse region geometry, Pareto-reverse direction, Pareto-vertex policy, Pareto-edge).

**3. Clarifications of Method Components.** Several reviewers asked for clearer definitions and distinctions:


(1) We formalized sparse regions as subsets of the objective space and provided clear algorithms for $m=2$, $3$, and $>3$ cases;

(2) We clarified that the Stage 3 include a heuristic but geometrically guided sparse-region detection. This heuristic algorithm is solely responsible for detecting sparse regions within the Tracked Pareto Front, rather than encompassing the entire “Sparse Region Filling” process;

(3) We explicitly distinguished MPFT’s temporary archive from evolutionary “populations,” demonstrating why our framework remains genuinely population-free.

**4. Comparison With Related Work (PGMORL, PA2D-MORL, C-MORL, GPI-LS, preference-conditioned methods).**Reviewers requested stronger comparisons with related paradigms and more evidence of novelty. We responded by:

(1) Adding comprehensive comparisons (Tables 3, please refer to page) with C-MORL, GPI-LS, and decomposition-based methods;

(2) Highlighting fundamental structural differences: no policy selection, no evolutionary loop, single-policy trackable updates, offline-RL compatibility, and controlled movement via Pareto-reverse;

(3) Showing MPFT achieves superior HV and EU under fewer interactions, including 9-objective tasks where GPI-LS requires >100 hours.

**5. Scalability, Experimental Breadth, and Ablations.** Reviewers raised concerns about lack of high-dimensional or discrete tasks, and ablation studies. We addressed them by:

(1) Adding new 4-D, 6-D, and 9-D environments (Lunar-Lander-4, Fruit-Tree-6, Building-9);

(2) Visualizing a 9-D tracked Pareto front (please to refer to page 4);

(3) Providing extensive ablations, and policy-buffer size (Appendix F.5, Tables 11-15, please refer to pages 35-37). Demonstrating MPFT’s robustness across parameter and environment variations.

**Final Statement.**

Through detail theory, clearer definitions, extended comparisons, substantial new experiments (including >3-objective and discrete domains), and strengthened complexity analysis, all concerns raised by reviewers ZLCd, yYLm, KQLK, and 7Ejc have been fully addressed. The revisions reinforce MPFT’s novelty, mathematical soundness, scalability, and superior efficiency compared with existing MP-MORL methods.
